              <h3>Interview with a Scrum Master</h3>
<p>Please see the following video interview about Scrum and the role of Scrum Master with Alex.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="uc_u5_s4_p2_scrum_interview" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="uc_u5_s4_p2_scrum_interview undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/uc_u5_s4_p2_scrum_interview.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/uc_u5_s4_p2_scrum_interview.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Alex:</strong> I'm Alex. I am a scrum master. I have been in the role for about six years. Definitely done a lot of learning on the job as well as formal qualification six years ago. But yeah, I'm just, I'm passionate about agile and agile development and continuous learning.</p>
<p>I used to work in marketing, I also came from a kind of traditional project management background. So did that for a number of years, but really adapting to change, which is the only constant really I've found in life so far. It's bringing in more of that kind of teamwork and collaboration and the kind of servant leadership element of it and protection of the team. I feel that that kind of really fits my, my purpose in life. And I'm quite big on kind of the meaning of names as well. And my name Alex actually means protector or defender of mankind. So I think that that fits quite nicely within my role as a scrum master and servant leader as well.</p>
<p>I think the main purpose of a scrum master is really being responsible for ensuring that the team lives the agile values and also kind of following the scrum process kind of being the protector of the team in that respect and removing obstacles and blockers, and really just establishing an effective working environment for the team as well. And, yeah, just doing everything that you can to make the team's life easier in order for them to be able to deliver the work that needs to be done.</p>
<p>I mean, scrum is obviously a kind of iterative product focused development, where you only commit to the work that the team plans to deliver within one sprint, which is up to a maximum of four weeks. As I said, previously, I came from a kind of traditional project management background and kind of comparing that way of working to the traditional waterfall way where you're kind of planning out the entire delivery from start to finish when inevitably things are going to change and you're going to come up against unknowns, not necessarily knowing the full requirements for a project, planning that, you know, from start to finish at the beginning, just doesn't really make much sense to me. And I think that's why agile works so well is that you're kind of chunking it down, just focusing on the short term and then really getting that kind of iterative development and feedback loops. So you're getting that fast feedback throughout the process from the stakeholders, from the end user, ideally to then develop the product even further.</p>
<p>Absolutely being the kind of servant leader for the team and really kind of protecting the product team ... To enable them to focus primarily on delivering the product. Helping to remove any blockers. Managing expectations with the key stakeholders and working with the product owner to develop the backlog and ensure that all of the stories and requirements are sprint ready in order to hand them over for the team to then commit to the work and deliver on those key requirements. But yeah, really kind of having that clear structure and being that owner of the process really, and yeah, protecting the team.</p>
<p>I think scrum is really effective in product development in general, whether that be software development in the digital arena. Definitely where a team is kind of all working towards the same goal. And where a team is really looking to kind of learn through their experiences and to continually improve their process together with that kind of collaboration. So as long as you've got a collaborative team, that's kind of working towards the same goal, then ultimately, hopefully scrum will work for you.</p>
<p>I think on the flip side of that, definitely without a self organising team or in a team that has no autonomy in terms of over their product delivery, that's where it's likely to fall down. I think it's really important to have a team that can... that can deliver the end to end product and is able to have that kind of autonomy and ownership of the product in order for them to deliver the best product, ultimately, really. So yeah, without a self-organising team it, yeah, it it's likely not to work.</p>
<p>I think just going back to that, to being that protector of the team you know having that clear structure, being the owner of the process and just making sure that we've got the right working environment for the team in order for them to do their job properly, removing any blockers, any issues and you know, that kind of full collaboration with the team, with the product owner, with the key stakeholders, making sure that you're kind of managing everyone's expectations and that you'v e got that clear pathway for the team to be able to do their job effectively.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
